    "lawCode": "Amd §1005, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8979\n\nSPONSOR: PERSAUD\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to requiring\nelectric vehicle charging stations and electric vehicle capable parking\nspaces to accommodate wheelchair accessible electric vehicles\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo require electric vehicle charging stations and electric vehicle capa-\nble parking spaces to accommodate wheelchair accessible vehicles.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends subdivision 9-a of section 1005 of the\npublic authorities law to require electric vehicle charging stations to\ninclude spaces that are large enough to accommodate wheelchair accessi-\nble electric vehicles, including but not limited to, vans and minivans.\n\nSection 2 of the bill sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nElectronic vehicles (EV'S) are becoming increasingly popular as New\nYorkers begin to conform to New York's climate goals and mandates; EV\ncharging stations should be accessible to all our state's citizens.\nThat is not the case for those individuals with disabilities and their\nneed for larger charging spaces depending on the type of vehicle. Just\nas there are designated spaces for ADA accessible parking for gas driven\nvehicles there should also be safe, easy access to EV charging stations\nfor wheelchair accessible vehicles.  The spaces should be labeled as ADA\naccessible EV charging stations and be large enough to accommodate the,\nvehicles used by New Yorkers with disabilities.\n\nMany car dealers maintain the EV charging stations themselves (Tesla)\nbut have also encourage property owners (shopping centers, office\ncomplexes etc.) to add these charging spaces. Currently, there is no\ndesignation of ADA accessible EV charging spaces that are set aside and\nthose who are driving ADA accessible vehicles are left to hope that the\nend of each station row is accessible. Too often, that is not the case.\nThis bill seeks to give priority usage of the end spaces to drivers with\ndisabilities.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPACT ON THE STATE:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
